COLOPHON
========

What this site is made of, and what it costs to keep answering you.


THE MACHINE

A Raspberry Pi 5 with 8GB of memory, on a desk in Brazil, plugged into a
domestic connection. nginx serves the static files and proxies /api to a
uvicorn on 127.0.0.1:8000; a Cloudflare Tunnel is what puts the whole thing on
the internet without opening a port on the router.

That is the entire hosting bill. `neofetch` and `top` read that board.


THE TERMINAL

xterm.js with the WebGL renderer, Vite and TypeScript, and no framework at
all -- there is no UI state for a React to manage, because there is no UI. The
parser for ANSI, the text selection, the line wrapping and the cursor come free
with xterm.js; the shell on top of it -- the lexer, the pipes, the redirects,
the globs, the line editor, the simulated filesystem -- is written for this
project and is about the size you would guess.

The API is FastAPI. It has five routes and its whole job is to read /proc and
hand it over as JSON.


DOOM

`doom` is the real game: doomgeneric compiled to WebAssembly with emscripten,
at 320x200, no SDL, no canvas and no sound. Each frame is drawn into the text
grid of the terminal itself -- two vertical pixels per character cell, in the
6x6x6 colour cube of a 256-colour terminal, with only the cells that changed
being repainted. The bottleneck is not the game. It is xterm.js swallowing the
bytes.


THE FONT

JetBrains Mono, self-hosted from the official release rather than from Google
Fonts, because the Google subsets do not carry the block and box-drawing
characters that the boot banner is entirely made of.


THE TREE

Every file you can `cat` lives as a plain .txt in the repository, under
content/en and content/pt, and is turned into the tree at build time. The dates
in `ls -l` are the dates of the commits that wrote those files. See /etc/fstab
for what is mounted from where.


WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

The numbers from the Pi are read at the moment you ask for them, and when they
cannot be -- because the machine is down, or because it is not a Linux box --
you are told, in the reply. No number here is decoration.

Nothing about you is stored beyond the first word of each command you type,
which /etc/privacy explains in full and in advance.


